const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_001","question":"A field is in the shape of an L. Its boundary consists of six straight sides meeting at right angles, and, read in order around the boundary, the sides measure 20 m, 14 m, 8 m, 5 m, 12 m and 9 m. Find the area of the field, in m².","options":{},"correct_answer":"220","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","compound_figures"],"common_errors":{"68":"Adding the six side lengths (the perimeter) instead of finding an area.","240":"Splitting into 20 × 9 and 12 × 5 but mis-reading which side bounds the upper block.","280":"Multiplying the first two sides (20 × 14) and treating the field as a full rectangle."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Split the L into two rectangles with a horizontal cut at the height of the shorter arm. Place a corner at the origin and walk the boundary: (0,0) → (20,0) → (20,14) → (12,14) → (12,9) → (0,9) → (0,0), which reproduces the six given lengths 20, 14, 8, 5, 12, 9 and closes. Lower rectangle: 20 × 9 = 180 m². Upper rectangle: 8 × 5 = 40 m². Total = 220 m². Check by the complement route: the full 20 × 14 = 280 rectangle minus the missing 12 × 5 = 60 corner = 220 m². Shoelace on the six vertices also gives 220 m²."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_002","question":"In trapezium $ABCD$, $AB \\\\parallel DC$ with $AB = 25$ cm and $DC = 13$ cm, and the two non-parallel sides satisfy $AD = BC = 10$ cm. Find the area of the trapezium, in cm².","options":{"A":"152","B":"190","C":"228","D":"76"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"microskills":["area_of_trapezium","pythagoras_theorem"],"common_errors":{"B":"Taking the slant side 10 cm as the height: ½(25 + 13)(10) = 190.","C":"Taking the horizontal offset 6 cm as the height and dropping the ½: (25 + 13)(6) = 228.","D":"Computing ¼(25 + 13)(8), i.e. halving twice."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Nothing can be computed until the height is constructed. Drop perpendiculars from D and C onto AB, meeting it at P and Q. Then PQ = DC = 13, and by symmetry (AD = BC) AP = QB = (25 − 13)/2 = 6 cm. In right triangle APD, h = √(10² − 6²) = √64 = 8 cm. Area = ½(AB + DC)h = ½(25 + 13)(8) = ½ × 38 × 8 = 152 cm². Second route: the trapezium is a 13 × 8 rectangle (104) plus two 6-8-10 right triangles (2 × 24 = 48); 104 + 48 = 152 cm²."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_003","question":"In quadrilateral $ABCD$, the diagonal $AC$ has length 20 cm. The perpendicular distance from $B$ to $AC$ is 6 cm and the perpendicular distance from $D$ to $AC$ is 9 cm. Find the area of the quadrilateral, in cm².","options":{},"correct_answer":"150","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["area_of_quadrilateral","area_of_triangle"],"common_errors":{"75":"Halving twice, or halving the sum of the offsets as well.","120":"Using only one of the two offsets (½ × 20 × 6 doubled, or a similar single-triangle slip).","300":"Using 20 × (6 + 9) without the factor ½."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The diagonal AC is the construction line: it splits ABCD into triangles ABC and ACD, both with AC as base. Area(ABC) = ½ × 20 × 6 = 60 cm². Area(ACD) = ½ × 20 × 9 = 90 cm². Total = 150 cm². Equivalently, area = ½ × d × (h₁ + h₂) = ½ × 20 × 15 = 150 cm²."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_004","question":"$ABCDEF$ is a regular hexagon of side 8 cm. Find the area of triangle $ACE$, in cm².","options":{"A":"$16\\\\sqrt{3}$","B":"$48\\\\sqrt{3}$","C":"$64\\\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$96\\\\sqrt{3}$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"microskills":["regular_polygon","area_of_equilateral_triangle"],"common_errors":{"A":"Treating ACE as equilateral of side 8 (the hexagon's side) rather than of side $8\\\\sqrt3$.","C":"Taking AC = 16 (twice the side) instead of $8\\\\sqrt3$.","D":"Giving the area of the whole hexagon instead of triangle ACE."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Join the centre O to all six vertices: the hexagon becomes six equilateral triangles of side 8, so its area is 6 × (√3/4)(8²) = 96√3 cm². For ACE, note that AC joins two vertices with one vertex between them; in triangle ABC, AB = BC = 8 and ∠ABC = 120°, so AC² = 64 + 64 − 2(64)cos120° = 192, AC = 8√3. By symmetry ACE is equilateral of side 8√3, so its area = (√3/4)(192) = 48√3 cm² ≈ 83.14. Cross-check by decomposition: the three corner triangles ABC, CDE, EFA are congruent, each of area ½(8)(8)sin120° = 16√3, totalling 48√3; hexagon 96√3 minus 48√3 = 48√3 cm² for ACE. The two routes agree."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_005","question":"A rectangular field measures 40 m by 25 m. Two straight paths, each 3 m wide, are laid across it: one runs the full length of the field parallel to its longer side, and the other runs the full width parallel to its shorter side, so that the two paths cross at right angles. Find the total area covered by the paths, in m².","options":{},"correct_answer":"186","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","compound_figures"],"common_errors":{"195":"Adding 40 × 3 and 25 × 3 without subtracting the 3 × 3 square counted twice.","204":"Adding the 3 × 3 overlap instead of subtracting it: 120 + 75 + 9 = 204.","1000":"Giving the area of the whole field."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"The two strips overlap in a 3 m × 3 m square, so the figure must be decomposed as strip + strip − overlap. Long strip: 40 × 3 = 120 m². Cross strip: 25 × 3 = 75 m². Overlap: 3 × 3 = 9 m². Paths = 120 + 75 − 9 = 186 m². Check by complement: the paths cut the field into four untouched rectangles whose total area is (40 − 3)(25 − 3) = 37 × 22 = 814 m², and 1000 − 814 = 186 m²."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_006","question":"In quadrilateral $ABCD$, $AB = 3$ cm, $BC = 4$ cm, $\\\\angle ABC = 90^\\\\circ$, $CD = 12$ cm and $DA = 13$ cm. Find the area of $ABCD$, in cm².","options":{"A":"30","B":"36","C":"78","D":"84"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"microskills":["area_of_quadrilateral","pythagoras_theorem"],"common_errors":{"A":"Finding only triangle ACD (30 cm²) and forgetting triangle ABC.","C":"Taking triangle ACD as right-angled with legs 12 and 13, giving 78, and dropping ABC.","D":"Taking triangle ACD as right-angled with legs 12 and 13 (78) and adding ABC (6)."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Draw the diagonal AC — that construction is the whole problem. In right triangle ABC, AC = √(3² + 4²) = 5 cm, and area(ABC) = ½ × 3 × 4 = 6 cm². Now triangle ACD has sides 5, 12, 13, and 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13², so it is right-angled at C: area(ACD) = ½ × 5 × 12 = 30 cm². Total = 6 + 30 = 36 cm². Check ACD by Heron's formula: s = 15, area = √(15 × 10 × 3 × 2) = √900 = 30 cm², confirming 36 cm²."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_007","question":"A shape is formed by taking a rectangle 20 cm long and 14 cm wide and attaching, externally on one of its 14 cm sides, a semicircle whose diameter is that side. Find the total area of the shape, in cm². (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"357","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","area_of_circle","compound_figures"],"common_errors":{"280":"Giving the rectangle's area only.","434":"Attaching a full circle of radius 7 instead of a semicircle (280 + 154).","588":"Using radius 14 instead of 7 for the semicircle (280 + 308)."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Decompose into rectangle + semicircle. Rectangle: 20 × 14 = 280 cm². The semicircle has diameter 14 cm, so radius 7 cm, and area ½πr² = ½ × (22/7) × 49 = 77 cm². Total = 280 + 77 = 357 cm²."},{"id":"ISO_AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION_008","question":"A rectangular garden measures 30 m by 20 m. A path of uniform width 2 m is laid inside the garden all along its boundary. Find the area of the path, in m².","options":{"A":"96","B":"184","C":"200","D":"216"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","compound_figures"],"common_errors":{"A":"Reducing each dimension by 2 m instead of 4 m: 600 − 28 × 18 = 96.","C":"Multiplying the perimeter by the width (100 × 2), which double-counts the four corners.","D":"Laying the path outside the garden: 34 × 24 − 600 = 216."},"target_procedural_skill":"AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","procedural_skills":["AUXILIARY_CONSTRUCTION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Decompose as outer rectangle minus inner rectangle. The path eats 2 m from each of the two opposite sides, so the inner lawn measures (30 − 4) by (20 − 4) = 26 × 16 = 416 m². Garden = 30 × 20 = 600 m². Path = 600 − 416 = 184 m². Second route: two long strips 30 × 2 = 60 each (120), two short strips 16 × 2 = 32 each (64); 120 + 64 = 184 m², the corners already accounted for by using the reduced 16 m length."},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_001","question":"A room is 12 m long, 9 m wide and 8 m high. Find the length, in metres, of the longest straight rod that can be placed inside it.","options":{},"correct_answer":"17","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["cuboid","space_diagonal"],"common_errors":{"15":"Using only the floor diagonal √(12² + 9²) = 15 and ignoring the height.","29":"Adding the three dimensions.","12.04":"Using √(9² + 8²) — a diagonal of one wall rather than of the room."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The longest rod lies along the space diagonal of the cuboid, from one corner to the opposite corner. Length = √(l² + b² + h²) = √(144 + 81 + 64) = √289 = 17 m. Second route: the floor diagonal is √(144 + 81) = 15 m, and the rod is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs 15 and 8, giving √(225 + 64) = 17 m."},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_002","question":"The largest possible sphere is carved out of a solid wooden cube of edge 12 cm. Find the volume of the sphere, in cm³.","options":{"A":"$144\\\\pi$","B":"$288\\\\pi$","C":"$864\\\\pi$","D":"$2304\\\\pi$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["sphere_volume","inscribed_solids"],"common_errors":{"A":"Computing the surface area 4πr² = 144π instead of the volume.","C":"Assuming the sphere occupies half the cube's volume (1728/2).","D":"Taking the radius as 12 cm (the edge) rather than 6 cm."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The largest sphere that fits in a cube touches all six faces, so its diameter equals the edge: d = 12 cm, r = 6 cm. Volume = (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)π(216) = 288π cm³ ≈ 904.78 cm³. (Sanity check: the cube's volume is 1728 cm³ and 288π ≈ 905, which is π/6 ≈ 52.4% of it — the standard sphere-in-cube fraction.)"},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_003","question":"The largest possible right circular cone is carved out of a solid right circular cylinder of base radius 7 cm and height 15 cm, the cone sharing the cylinder's base. Find the volume of the cone, in cm³. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"770","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cone_volume","cylinder_volume","inscribed_solids"],"common_errors":{"385":"Halving the cylinder's volume rather than taking a third.","1540":"Using ⅔πr²h, or subtracting the cone from the cylinder (the waste, not the cone).","2310":"Giving the cylinder's volume, i.e. forgetting the factor 1/3."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The largest cone inside the cylinder has the same base radius and the same height as the cylinder: r = 7 cm, h = 15 cm. Volume = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)(22/7)(49)(15) = (1/3)(2310) = 770 cm³. (The cylinder's volume is 2310 cm³, and the cone is exactly one third of it.)"},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_004","question":"A cube of the largest possible volume is cut from a solid sphere of radius $6\\\\sqrt{3}$ cm. Find the volume of the cube, in cm³.","options":{"A":"$648\\\\sqrt{3}$","B":"1728","C":"$1296\\\\sqrt{6}$","D":"$5184\\\\sqrt{3}$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"microskills":["cube_volume","sphere","inscribed_solids"],"common_errors":{"A":"Taking the cube's edge to be the radius $6\\\\sqrt3$.","C":"Equating the sphere's diameter with a face diagonal $a\\\\sqrt2$ instead of the space diagonal $a\\\\sqrt3$.","D":"Taking the cube's edge to be the diameter $12\\\\sqrt3$."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"For the largest inscribed cube, all eight vertices lie on the sphere, so the cube's space diagonal equals the sphere's diameter. Diameter = 2 × 6√3 = 12√3 cm. For a cube of edge a, the space diagonal is a√3, so a√3 = 12√3, giving a = 12 cm. Volume = 12³ = 1728 cm³. Check: with a = 12, the space diagonal is √(144 + 144 + 144) = √432 = 12√3 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_005","question":"A right-angled triangle whose legs measure 7 cm and 12 cm is rotated through a full turn about its 12 cm leg. Find the volume of the solid generated, in cm³. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"616","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cone_volume","solid_of_revolution"],"common_errors":{"1056":"Rotating about the 7 cm leg instead: (1/3)π(12²)(7) = 1056.","1232":"Doubling the cone, as if both legs were swept.","1848":"Using πr²h (a cylinder) instead of (1/3)πr²h."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Rotating a right triangle about one leg sweeps out a right circular cone whose axis is that leg. Rotating about the 12 cm leg makes h = 12 cm and the other leg becomes the base radius, r = 7 cm. Volume = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)(22/7)(49)(12) = (1/3)(1848) = 616 cm³. (Had the rotation been about the 7 cm leg, r = 12 and h = 7 would give 1056 cm³ — the two are different, which is the point of the visualisation.)"},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_006","question":"Two solid cubes, each of edge 6 cm, are joined face to face to form a single solid. Find the total surface area of the resulting solid, in cm².","options":{"A":"288","B":"360","C":"396","D":"432"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cuboid_surface_area","cube"],"common_errors":{"A":"Counting only the four 12 × 6 rectangular faces (4 × 72 = 288) and omitting the two 6 × 6 end squares.","C":"Subtracting only one of the two joined faces (432 − 36).","D":"Adding the two cubes' surface areas (2 × 216) with no faces lost at the join."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Joining the cubes face to face produces a cuboid 12 cm × 6 cm × 6 cm; the two faces at the join are now internal and are not part of the surface. Surface area = 2(lb + bh + lh) = 2(12×6 + 6×6 + 12×6) = 2(72 + 36 + 72) = 360 cm². Second route: the two cubes have 12 square faces of 36 cm² in total, of which 2 are lost at the join; the remaining 10 faces give 10 × 36 = 360 cm². The two routes agree."},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_007","question":"The largest possible right circular cylinder is carved out of a solid wooden cube of edge 14 cm, the cylinder's axis being perpendicular to two opposite faces of the cube. Find the volume of the cylinder, in cm³. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"2156","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","inscribed_solids"],"common_errors":{"588":"Giving the waste (cube minus cylinder) or another mis-substitution.","2744":"Giving the cube's volume.","8624":"Taking the radius as 14 cm (the edge) instead of 7 cm."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"The largest such cylinder has its circular cross-section inscribed in a face of the cube, so its diameter equals the edge: r = 7 cm; and its height spans the cube: h = 14 cm. Volume = πr²h = (22/7)(49)(14) = 22 × 7 × 14 = 2156 cm³. (The cube is 2744 cm³, so the cylinder is π/4 ≈ 78.5% of it — the standard fraction ✓.)"},{"id":"ISO_SPATIAL_VISUALISATION_008","question":"A solid is formed by fixing a hemisphere of radius 6 cm exactly on top of the flat circular top face of a solid right circular cylinder of radius 6 cm and height 10 cm. Find the total surface area of the solid, in cm².","options":{"A":"$192\\\\pi$","B":"$228\\\\pi$","C":"$264\\\\pi$","D":"$300\\\\pi$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"microskills":["cylinder_surface_area","hemisphere","composite_solids"],"common_errors":{"A":"Omitting the cylinder's bottom circular face.","C":"Counting the cylinder's top circle even though the hemisphere covers it.","D":"Using the full sphere's surface 4πr² for the hemisphere instead of 2πr²."},"target_procedural_skill":"SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","procedural_skills":["SPATIAL_VISUALISATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Visualise which surfaces are exposed. The cylinder contributes its curved surface 2πrh = 2π(6)(10) = 120π and its bottom circular face πr² = 36π; its top circle is sealed by the hemisphere and is not exposed. The hemisphere contributes only its curved surface 2πr² = 72π (its flat face is the join). Total = 120π + 36π + 72π = 228π cm² ≈ 716.28 cm²."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_001","question":"The areas of two similar triangles are in the ratio $9 : 16$. The perimeter of the smaller triangle is 45 cm. Find the perimeter of the larger triangle, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"60","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["similar_triangles","ratio_of_areas"],"common_errors":{"80":"Scaling the perimeter by the area ratio 16/9 instead of by the length ratio 4/3.","180":"Scaling by the square root of 16 alone (×4).","33.75":"Scaling downwards by 3/4 rather than upwards by 4/3."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Perimeter is a length, so it scales as k, while area scales as k². Here k² = 16/9, so k = 4/3. Larger perimeter = 45 × 4/3 = 60 cm. Check: the perimeters 45 and 60 are in the ratio 3 : 4, and squaring gives 9 : 16 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_002","question":"The volumes of two cubes are in the ratio $27 : 125$. Find the ratio of their total surface areas.","options":{"A":"$3 : 5$","B":"$9 : 25$","C":"$27 : 125$","D":"$81 : 625$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["cube","ratio_of_areas","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"A":"Giving the ratio of edges (k) rather than of areas (k²).","C":"Assuming surface area scales the same way as volume.","D":"Squaring the volume ratio instead of taking the cube root then squaring."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING"],"solution":"Volume scales as k³, so k³ = 27/125 gives k = 3/5 for the edges. Surface area scales as k² = 9/25. So the total surface areas are in the ratio 9 : 25. Check with actual cubes: edges 3 and 5 give volumes 27 and 125 ✓, and surface areas 54 and 150, which is 9 : 25 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_003","question":"A right circular cone is cut by a plane parallel to its base, passing through the midpoint of its axis. Find the ratio of the volume of the small cone so removed to the volume of the remaining frustum.","options":{"A":"$1 : 2$","B":"$1 : 4$","C":"$1 : 7$","D":"$1 : 8$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cone_volume","frustum","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"A":"Assuming the halved axis halves the volume.","B":"Using k² rather than k³, then subtracting.","D":"Giving the ratio of the small cone to the whole cone instead of to the frustum."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"The small cone is similar to the whole cone with linear scale factor k = 1/2, so its volume is k³ = 1/8 of the whole. Taking the whole cone as 8 units, the small cone is 1 and the frustum is 8 − 1 = 7. Ratio = 1 : 7. Check with numbers: a cone of r = 4, h = 8 has volume (1/3)π(16)(8) = 128π/3; the top cone has r = 2, h = 4 and volume (1/3)π(4)(4) = 16π/3; the frustum is 112π/3, and 16 : 112 = 1 : 7 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_004","question":"A scale model of a ship is built to a scale of $1 : 40$. The deck of the model has an area of 1.5 m². Find the area of the actual deck, in m².","options":{},"correct_answer":"2400","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["scale_models","ratio_of_areas"],"common_errors":{"60":"Scaling the area by 40 instead of by 40².","96000":"Scaling the area by 40³, the volume factor.","0.0375":"Dividing by 40 instead of multiplying."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Lengths scale by 40, so areas scale by 40² = 1600. Actual deck area = 1.5 × 1600 = 2400 m². Check: a model deck of, say, 2 m × 0.75 m = 1.5 m² corresponds to an actual deck of 80 m × 30 m = 2400 m² ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_005","question":"Each side of a rectangle is increased by 20%. By what percentage does its area increase?","options":{"A":"20%","B":"40%","C":"44%","D":"144%"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["percentage_change","area_of_rectangle","ratio_of_areas"],"common_errors":{"A":"Applying the linear factor directly to the area.","B":"Adding the two 20% increases.","D":"Giving the new area as a percentage of the old, not the increase."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Each length is multiplied by k = 1.2, so the area is multiplied by k² = 1.44. The increase is 0.44, i.e. 44%. Check with numbers: a 10 × 5 rectangle (area 50) becomes 12 × 6 (area 72); (72 − 50)/50 = 44% ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_006","question":"The radius of a sphere is decreased by 10%. Find the percentage decrease in its volume, correct to one decimal place.","options":{},"correct_answer":"27.1","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["sphere_volume","percentage_change","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"10":"Applying the linear percentage directly to the volume.","19":"Using k² (0.81), the surface-area factor.","30":"Multiplying the 10% decrease by 3 instead of using 0.9³."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Volume scales as k³ with k = 0.9. New volume factor = 0.9³ = 0.729, so the decrease is 1 − 0.729 = 0.271, i.e. 27.1%. Check with numbers: r = 10 gives V = (4/3)π(1000) = 4188.79; r = 9 gives V = (4/3)π(729) = 3053.63; the drop is 1135.16, which is 27.1% of 4188.79 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_007","question":"In triangle $ABC$, $D$ lies on $AB$ and $E$ lies on $AC$ with $DE \\\\parallel BC$ and $AD : DB = 2 : 3$. Find the ratio of the area of triangle $ADE$ to the area of trapezium $DBCE$.","options":{"A":"$2 : 3$","B":"$4 : 9$","C":"$4 : 21$","D":"$4 : 25$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":105,"microskills":["similar_triangles","ratio_of_areas","basic_proportionality"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using the length ratio AD : DB directly as an area ratio.","B":"Squaring AD : DB (2 : 3) instead of AD : AB (2 : 5).","D":"Giving triangle ADE : triangle ABC rather than ADE : trapezium."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Since DE ∥ BC, triangles ADE and ABC are similar with linear scale factor k = AD/AB = 2/(2 + 3) = 2/5. Areas scale as k² = 4/25, so taking area(ABC) = 25 units, area(ADE) = 4 and the trapezium DBCE = 25 − 4 = 21. Ratio = 4 : 21. The classic error is to use AD : DB = 2 : 3 as the scale factor; the scale factor must compare the full corresponding sides AD and AB."},{"id":"ISO_SIMILARITY_SCALING_008","question":"Two right circular cylinders are similar (one is a scale copy of the other). Their curved surface areas are in the ratio $16 : 49$. Find the ratio of their volumes.","options":{"A":"$4 : 7$","B":"$16 : 49$","C":"$64 : 343$","D":"$256 : 2401$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","ratio_of_areas","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"A":"Giving the linear ratio k rather than k³.","B":"Assuming volume scales like area.","D":"Squaring the area ratio instead of converting to k and cubing."},"target_procedural_skill":"SIMILARITY_SCALING","procedural_skills":["SIMILARITY_SCALING"],"solution":"Areas scale as k², so k² = 16/49 gives k = 4/7. Volumes scale as k³ = 64/343. Ratio = 64 : 343. Check with numbers: cylinders (r = 4, h = 4) and (r = 7, h = 7) have curved surface areas 32π and 98π, in the ratio 16 : 49 ✓, and volumes 64π and 343π, in the ratio 64 : 343 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_001","question":"A rectangular sheet of metal measures 1.2 m by 80 cm. Find its area, in cm².","options":{},"correct_answer":"9600","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","unit_conversion"],"common_errors":{"96":"Multiplying 1.2 by 80 without converting the metres to centimetres.","960":"Converting 1.2 m to 12 cm instead of 120 cm.","0.96":"Working in m² (1.2 × 0.8) but reporting the answer as if it were cm²."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert first: 1.2 m = 120 cm. Area = 120 × 80 = 9600 cm². Cross-check via m²: 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96 m², and 1 m² = 10 000 cm², so 0.96 × 10 000 = 9600 cm² ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_002","question":"A cuboidal water tank is 2 m long, 1.5 m wide and 80 cm deep. Find its capacity, in litres.","options":{},"correct_answer":"2400","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["cuboid_volume","unit_conversion","capacity"],"common_errors":{"240":"Using 2 × 1.5 × 0.8 = 2.4 m³ and then multiplying by 100 instead of 1000.","2400000":"Giving the volume in cm³ rather than litres.","2.4":"Stopping at the volume in m³ without converting to litres."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert the depth: 80 cm = 0.8 m. Volume = 2 × 1.5 × 0.8 = 2.4 m³. Since 1 m³ = 1000 litres, capacity = 2400 litres. Cross-check in centimetres: 200 × 150 × 80 = 2 400 000 cm³, and 1 litre = 1000 cm³, giving 2400 litres ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_003","question":"A piece of wire 3.6 m long is bent to form a square. Find the length of each side of the square, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"90","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["perimeter_of_square","unit_conversion"],"common_errors":{"9":"Converting 3.6 m to 36 cm instead of 360 cm.","900":"Dividing 3600 mm by 4 and reporting it as cm.","0.9":"Working in metres but reporting the answer as centimetres."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert first: 3.6 m = 360 cm. The wire becomes the perimeter of the square, so side = 360/4 = 90 cm. Cross-check: 0.9 m × 4 = 3.6 m ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_004","question":"During a storm, 5 mm of rain falls on a flat rectangular roof measuring 40 m by 25 m. Find the volume of rainwater collected, in litres.","options":{},"correct_answer":"5000","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cuboid_volume","unit_conversion","capacity"],"common_errors":{"5":"Stopping at the volume in m³ without converting to litres.","50":"Converting 5 mm to 0.05 m instead of 0.005 m.","5000000":"Treating 5 mm as 5 m, or converting m³ to cm³ and calling it litres."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert the depth of rain: 5 mm = 0.005 m. Volume = 40 × 25 × 0.005 = 5 m³. Since 1 m³ = 1000 litres, the water collected is 5000 litres. Cross-check in centimetres: 4000 × 2500 × 0.5 = 5 000 000 cm³ = 5000 litres ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_005","question":"A solid cube has edge 20 cm. Find its total surface area, in m².","options":{"A":"0.024","B":"0.24","C":"2.4","D":"24"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["cube_surface_area","unit_conversion"],"common_errors":{"A":"Dividing the 2400 cm² by 100 000, or another power-of-ten slip.","C":"Dividing 2400 cm² by 1000 instead of 10 000.","D":"Dividing by 100 (the length conversion factor) instead of 100²."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Surface area = 6a² = 6 × 20² = 2400 cm². Since 1 m = 100 cm, 1 m² = 10 000 cm², so 2400 cm² = 2400/10 000 = 0.24 m². Cross-check by converting first: a = 0.2 m, surface area = 6 × 0.04 = 0.24 m² ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_006","question":"A cylindrical vessel has radius 3.5 cm and height 0.2 m. Find its volume, in cm³. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{"A":"77","B":"154","C":"770","D":"7700"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","unit_conversion"],"common_errors":{"A":"Taking the height as 2 cm instead of 20 cm.","B":"Using 0.2 m as 4 cm, or a mis-squared radius.","D":"Taking the height as 200 cm."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert the height: 0.2 m = 20 cm. Volume = πr²h = (22/7)(3.5)²(20) = (22/7)(12.25)(20) = 770 cm³. Cross-check: (22/7) × 12.25 = 38.5, and 38.5 × 20 = 770 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_007","question":"A solid brick wall measures 6 m long, 1.2 m high and 0.4 m thick. It is built entirely from bricks each measuring 25 cm by 12 cm by 8 cm, with no mortar. How many bricks are used?","options":{"A":"120","B":"1200","C":"1500","D":"12000"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cuboid_volume","unit_conversion"],"common_errors":{"A":"A factor-of-ten slip in converting the wall's dimensions to centimetres.","C":"Using a brick volume of 1920 cm³ (e.g. 20 × 12 × 8).","D":"Converting metres to centimetres in only some of the three dimensions."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert the wall to centimetres: 600 cm × 120 cm × 40 cm = 2 880 000 cm³. Each brick is 25 × 12 × 8 = 2400 cm³. Number of bricks = 2 880 000 / 2400 = 1200. Cross-check in metres: wall = 6 × 1.2 × 0.4 = 2.88 m³; brick = 0.25 × 0.12 × 0.08 = 0.0024 m³; 2.88/0.0024 = 1200 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_UNIT_CONVERSION_008","question":"A square field has an area of 2.25 hectares. Find the length of one side of the field, in metres. (1 hectare = 10 000 m².)","options":{},"correct_answer":"150","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["area_of_square","unit_conversion"],"common_errors":{"15":"Using 1 hectare = 100 m².","22500":"Giving the area in m² instead of the side length.","1.5":"Taking the square root of 2.25 without converting hectares to square metres."},"target_procedural_skill":"UNIT_CONVERSION","procedural_skills":["UNIT_CONVERSION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Convert first: 2.25 hectares = 2.25 × 10 000 = 22 500 m². Side = √22 500 = 150 m. Cross-check: 150² = 22 500 m² = 2.25 hectares ✓."},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_001","question":"An open rectangular water tank (that is, one with no lid) is 3 m long, 2 m wide and 1.5 m deep. Its inside is to be completely lined with tiles — the base and all four walls. Find the area to be tiled, in m².","options":{},"correct_answer":"21","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cuboid_surface_area","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"9":"Counting only one wall of each pair.","15":"Tiling the four walls only and forgetting the base.","27":"Including the top as well, i.e. treating the tank as a closed box."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Translate: an open tank means five faces — one base plus four walls. Base = 3 × 2 = 6 m². The two longer walls: 2 × (3 × 1.5) = 9 m². The two shorter walls: 2 × (2 × 1.5) = 6 m². Total = 6 + 9 + 6 = 21 m². Second route: lb + 2h(l + b) = 6 + 2(1.5)(5) = 6 + 15 = 21 m² ✓."},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_002","question":"A road roller is in the shape of a right circular cylinder of diameter 1.4 m and length 2 m. Find the area of road it levels in one complete revolution, in m². (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{"A":"1.54","B":"4.4","C":"8.8","D":"17.6"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cylinder_surface_area","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using the circular cross-sectional area πr² = (22/7)(0.7)² = 1.54 instead of the curved surface.","B":"Giving the circumference (πd) alone, forgetting the roller's 2 m length.","D":"Using πdL with the diameter mistaken for the radius."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Translate: in one revolution the roller's curved surface rolls once over the road, so the area levelled equals the roller's curved surface area. CSA = 2πrL = πdL = (22/7)(1.4)(2) = 8.8 m². Cross-check: circumference = (22/7)(1.4) = 4.4 m, and 4.4 × 2 = 8.8 m² ✓."},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_003","question":"A conical tent has a base radius of 7 m and a slant height of 25 m. Find the area of canvas required to make the tent, in m², assuming the tent has no floor and no canvas is wasted. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"550","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cone_surface_area","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"528":"Using the vertical height 24 m in place of the slant height.","704":"Including the base circle: 550 + 154.","1100":"Using 2πrl instead of πrl."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Translate: a floorless tent is exactly the curved surface of the cone. Canvas = πrl = (22/7)(7)(25) = 22 × 25 = 550 m². (Note the vertical height, √(25² − 7²) = 24 m, is not needed — the slant height is what the curved surface uses.)"},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_004","question":"A rectangular sheet of paper measuring 22 cm by 12 cm is rolled along its length, without overlap, to form the curved surface of an open cylinder. Find the radius of the cylinder, in cm. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{"A":"1.75","B":"3.5","C":"7","D":"11"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cylinder","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"A":"Halving the correct radius, i.e. treating 3.5 as the diameter.","C":"Taking 22 cm to be the circumference but solving 22 = πr rather than 22 = 2πr.","D":"Taking 22 cm to be the diameter of the cylinder."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Translate: rolling along the length means the 22 cm side wraps around to become the circumference, and the 12 cm side becomes the height. So 2πr = 22, giving r = 22/(2 × 22/7) = 22 × 7/44 = 3.5 cm. Check: 2 × (22/7) × 3.5 = 22 cm ✓."},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_005","question":"A field is in the shape of a right-angled triangle whose two perpendicular sides measure 30 m and 40 m. A fence is to be erected along the entire boundary of the field. Find the total length of fencing required, in metres.","options":{},"correct_answer":"120","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["pythagoras_theorem","perimeter","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"70":"Fencing only the two perpendicular sides and omitting the hypotenuse.","100":"Using an incorrect hypotenuse of 30 m.","600":"Giving the area of the field instead of its perimeter."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Translate: 'fence along the entire boundary' means the perimeter, so the third side is needed. Hypotenuse = √(30² + 40²) = √2500 = 50 m. Perimeter = 30 + 40 + 50 = 120 m. (The 30-40-50 triangle is a scaled 3-4-5 ✓.)"},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_006","question":"A cubical box with internal edge 20 cm is packed completely full with small solid cubical blocks, each of edge 5 cm, leaving no gaps. How many blocks does the box hold?","options":{},"correct_answer":"64","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["cube_volume","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"4":"Dividing only the edges (20/5) and stopping.","16":"Squaring rather than cubing the edge ratio — filling one layer only.","1600":"Dividing the box's volume by the block's EDGE rather than its volume: 8000/5 = 1600."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Translate: filling a solid box means the block count is the volume ratio. Box volume = 20³ = 8000 cm³; block volume = 5³ = 125 cm³; 8000/125 = 64. Second route: 20/5 = 4 blocks along each edge, so 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 blocks ✓."},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_007","question":"A room is 8 m long, 6 m wide and 4 m high. The four walls are to be painted at a cost of ₹25 per m². Find the total cost of painting, in rupees. (Ignore doors and windows.)","options":{"A":"₹1400","B":"₹2800","C":"₹4000","D":"₹5600"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cuboid_surface_area","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"A":"Counting only one wall of each pair (56 m² × 25).","C":"Including the ceiling as well: (112 + 48) × 25.","D":"Including both the ceiling and the floor, or double-counting the walls."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","NUMERIC_EXECUTION"],"solution":"Translate: 'the four walls' means the lateral surface area only — no floor, no ceiling. Lateral area = 2h(l + b) = 2 × 4 × (8 + 6) = 8 × 14 = 112 m². Cost = 112 × 25 = ₹2800. Second route: two walls of 8 × 4 = 32 (total 64) and two of 6 × 4 = 24 (total 48); 64 + 48 = 112 m² ✓."},{"id":"ISO_WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION_008","question":"A hollow iron pipe is 20 cm long. Its external radius is 7 cm and its internal radius is 3.5 cm. Find the volume of iron in the pipe, in cm³. (Take $\\\\pi = \\\\frac{22}{7}$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"2310","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","hollow_cylinder","real_world_modelling"],"common_errors":{"770":"Giving the volume of the hollow interior instead of the iron.","3080":"Giving the volume of the whole outer cylinder, ignoring the hollow.","2887.5":"Reading 3.5 cm as the internal DIAMETER, so r = 1.75: π(49 − 3.0625)(20) = 2887.5."},"target_procedural_skill":"WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","procedural_skills":["WORD_PROBLEM_TRANSLATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Translate: 'the volume of iron' is the outer cylinder minus the hollow core. V = π(R² − r²)h = (22/7)(49 − 12.25)(20) = (22/7)(36.75)(20) = 115.5 × 20 = 2310 cm³. Cross-check separately: outer = (22/7)(49)(20) = 3080 cm³; inner = (22/7)(12.25)(20) = 770 cm³; 3080 − 770 = 2310 cm³ ✓."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_001","question":"The radii of two right circular cylinders are in the ratio $2 : 3$ and their heights are in the ratio $5 : 4$. Find the ratio of their volumes.","options":{"A":"$5 : 6$","B":"$5 : 9$","C":"$8 : 27$","D":"$10 : 27$"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"A":"Using r rather than r²: (2×5) : (3×4) = 10 : 12 = 5 : 6.","C":"Cubing the radius ratio and ignoring the heights.","D":"Mixing r² for one cylinder with r for the other."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"Volume ∝ r²h, so the ratio is (2² × 5) : (3² × 4) = 20 : 36 = 5 : 9. No actual dimensions are needed, and none can be recovered."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_002","question":"Two right circular cones have base radii in the ratio $3 : 4$ and heights in the ratio $8 : 9$. Find the ratio of their volumes.","options":{"A":"$1 : 2$","B":"$2 : 3$","C":"$3 : 4$","D":"$27 : 64$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["cone_volume","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"B":"Using r rather than r²: (3×8) : (4×9) = 24 : 36 = 2 : 3.","C":"Giving the ratio of radii and ignoring the heights.","D":"Cubing the radius ratio, as if the cones were similar."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"Volume ∝ r²h, so the ratio is (3² × 8) : (4² × 9) = 72 : 144 = 1 : 2. Note the cones are not similar (3 : 4 ≠ 8 : 9), so no single scale factor applies — each dimension must be weighted by its own power."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_003","question":"A right circular cylinder and a right circular cone have equal base radii and equal heights. Find the ratio of the volume of the cylinder to the volume of the cone.","options":{"A":"$1 : 3$","B":"$2 : 1$","C":"$3 : 1$","D":"$1 : 2$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","cone_volume","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"A":"Inverting the ratio — giving cone : cylinder.","B":"Confusing the cone's 1/3 factor with a 1/2 factor.","D":"Both inverting and using 1/2."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"With the same r and h, cylinder = πr²h and cone = (1/3)πr²h. The common factor πr²h cancels, leaving 1 : 1/3 = 3 : 1. The absolute volumes are never needed."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_004","question":"In triangle $ABC$, the point $D$ lies on side $BC$ with $BD : DC = 3 : 4$, and $E$ is the midpoint of $AD$. Find the ratio of the area of triangle $BEC$ to the area of triangle $ABC$.","options":{"A":"$1 : 2$","B":"$1 : 3$","C":"$3 : 7$","D":"$3 : 14$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":135,"microskills":["area_of_triangle","ratio_of_areas"],"common_errors":{"B":"Assuming the midpoint splits the area into three equal parts.","C":"Using BD : BC = 3 : 7, i.e. answering about triangle ABD instead.","D":"Combining 3 : 7 with a further halving."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"Compare areas by base and height rather than computing any of them. Triangles BED and BAD share the vertex B and have bases ED and AD on the same line with ED = ½AD, so area(BED) = ½ area(BAD). Identically, area(DEC) = ½ area(DAC). Adding: area(BEC) = ½[area(BAD) + area(DAC)] = ½ area(ABC). Ratio = 1 : 2. Note BD : DC = 3 : 4 is irrelevant — verified by coordinates with several different triangles and several different values of BD : DC, all giving exactly 1/2. Equivalently, E is at half the perpendicular height of A above BC, and BEC shares the base BC."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_005","question":"The length of a rectangle is increased in the ratio $5 : 4$ and its breadth is decreased in the ratio $2 : 3$. In what ratio does its area change?","options":{"A":"$5 : 6$","B":"$6 : 5$","C":"$10 : 7$","D":"$15 : 8$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","ratio_of_change"],"common_errors":{"B":"Inverting — giving old area : new area.","C":"Adding the ratios instead of multiplying them.","D":"Multiplying 5/4 by 3/2, i.e. increasing the breadth instead of decreasing it."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"'Increased in the ratio 5 : 4' multiplies the length by 5/4; 'decreased in the ratio 2 : 3' multiplies the breadth by 2/3. Area is multiplied by (5/4)(2/3) = 10/12 = 5/6. So new : old = 5 : 6 — the area falls. No dimensions are needed."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_006","question":"Two right circular cylinders have equal volumes, and their radii are in the ratio $3 : 2$. Find the ratio of their heights.","options":{"A":"$2 : 3$","B":"$3 : 2$","C":"$4 : 9$","D":"$9 : 4$"},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cylinder_volume","inverse_proportion","ratio_of_volumes"],"common_errors":{"A":"Inverting the radii linearly rather than inverting their squares.","B":"Taking the heights to be in the same ratio as the radii.","D":"Using the ratio of squares directly instead of inverting it."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"Equal volumes means r²h is the same for both, so h ∝ 1/r². With radii 3 : 2, the heights are in the ratio (1/9) : (1/4) = 4 : 9. Check with numbers: r = 3, h = 4 gives 36π; r = 2, h = 9 gives 36π — equal ✓."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_007","question":"For a right circular cylinder, the ratio of its curved surface area to its total surface area is $2 : 5$. Find the ratio of its height to its radius.","options":{"A":"$2 : 3$","B":"$3 : 2$","C":"$2 : 5$","D":"$5 : 2$"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"microskills":["cylinder_surface_area","ratio_reasoning"],"common_errors":{"B":"Inverting — giving r : h.","C":"Restating the given ratio as the answer.","D":"Inverting the given ratio."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"CSA = 2πrh and TSA = 2πr(h + r). Their ratio is h/(h + r) = 2/5. Cross-multiplying: 5h = 2h + 2r, so 3h = 2r, giving h : r = 2 : 3. Check: with r = 3 and h = 2, CSA = 2π(3)(2) = 12π and TSA = 2π(3)(5) = 30π, and 12 : 30 = 2 : 5 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_RATIO_REASONING_008","question":"A right circular cone and a sphere have the same radius, and their volumes are equal. Find the ratio of the height of the cone to its radius. (Give your answer as the value of $h/r$.)","options":{},"correct_answer":"4","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cone_volume","sphere_volume","ratio_reasoning"],"common_errors":{"2":"Cancelling the 1/3 against the 4/3 incorrectly and getting h = 2r.","3":"Inverting, or using (4/3) ÷ (4/9).","1.33":"Reading off 4/3 directly without dividing by the cone's 1/3."},"target_procedural_skill":"RATIO_REASONING","procedural_skills":["RATIO_REASONING"],"solution":"Set (1/3)πr²h = (4/3)πr³. The common factor (1/3)πr² cancels, leaving h = 4r, so h/r = 4. The radius itself is never determined and never needs to be."},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_001","question":"In triangle $ABC$, the point $D$ lies on side $BC$ with $AD \\\\perp BC$. Given that $AB = 15$ cm, $AD = 12$ cm and $DC = 5$ cm, find the length of $BC$, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"14","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"microskills":["pythagoras_theorem","altitude"],"common_errors":{"4":"Subtracting DC from BD instead of adding, i.e. placing D outside BC.","9":"Giving BD and forgetting to add DC.","13":"Computing AC = √(AD² + DC²) = √169 = 13 and reporting it as BC."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration: D is between B and C, so BC = BD + DC. Triangle ABD is right-angled at D, so BD = √(AB² − AD²) = √(225 − 144) = √81 = 9 cm. Hence BC = 9 + 5 = 14 cm. (For interest, AC = √(144 + 25) = 13 cm, so the triangle has sides 15, 13, 14.)"},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_002","question":"In triangle $ABC$, $\\\\angle ABC = 90^\\\\circ$, and $D$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $B$ to the hypotenuse $AC$. Given that $AD = 4$ cm and $DC = 9$ cm, find the length of $BD$, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"6","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["pythagoras_theorem","similar_triangles","altitude_on_hypotenuse"],"common_errors":{"13":"Giving AC rather than BD.","36":"Stopping at BD² = 4 × 9 = 36 and reporting it without taking the square root.","6.5":"Halving AC (the median to the hypotenuse) instead of using the altitude."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration: the altitude from the right angle to the hypotenuse creates two triangles ABD and BCD each similar to ABC and to each other. Hence AD/BD = BD/DC, so BD² = AD × DC = 4 × 9 = 36, giving BD = 6 cm. Check the whole figure: AC = 13, AB = √(AD × AC) = √52 = 2√13, BC = √(DC × AC) = √117 = 3√13; then AB² + BC² = 52 + 117 = 169 = 13² ✓, and ½(AB)(BC) = ½(2√13)(3√13) = 39 = ½(13)(6) ✓."},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_003","question":"In a circle with centre $O$, $AB$ is a chord of length 16 cm. The perpendicular drawn from $O$ to $AB$ meets it at $M$, and $OM = 6$ cm. Find the radius of the circle, in cm.","options":{"A":"8","B":"10","C":"$2\\\\sqrt{73}$","D":"17"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["circle_chord_properties","pythagoras_theorem"],"common_errors":{"A":"Giving half the chord (AM) instead of the radius.","C":"Using the full chord 16 as a leg: √(256 + 36) = 2√73.","D":"Adding the chord and the distance, or another mis-formed triangle."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration: the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects it, so AM = 16/2 = 8 cm. Triangle OMA is right-angled at M, so OA = √(OM² + AM²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10 cm. The radius is 10 cm. (The 6-8-10 triangle is a scaled 3-4-5 ✓.)"},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_004","question":"Two circles, of radii 9 cm and 4 cm, touch each other internally. Find the distance between their centres, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"5","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["circle_tangency"],"common_errors":{"13":"Giving the answer for circles touching externally (9 + 4).","36":"Multiplying the radii.","6.5":"Averaging the radii."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION"],"solution":"Read the configuration: when two circles touch internally, the smaller lies inside the larger and the point of contact is collinear with both centres, on the same side. So the distance between the centres is the difference of the radii: 9 − 4 = 5 cm. (Had they touched externally the answer would be the sum, 13 cm — recognising which is the whole task.)"},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_005","question":"$ABCD$ is a parallelogram in which $AD = 7$ cm and $DC = 11$ cm. The bisector of $\\\\angle A$ meets side $DC$ at the point $E$. Find the length of $EC$, in cm.","options":{"A":"4","B":"5.5","C":"7","D":"11"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":135,"microskills":["parallelogram_properties","angle_bisector","isosceles_triangle"],"common_errors":{"B":"Assuming the bisector meets DC at its midpoint.","C":"Giving DE (which equals AD = 7) instead of EC.","D":"Giving DC itself."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration. In parallelogram ABCD, AB ∥ DC. Since AE bisects ∠A, ∠DAE = ∠EAB. Because AB ∥ DC and AE is a transversal, ∠EAB = ∠AED (alternate angles). Therefore ∠DAE = ∠AED, so triangle ADE is isosceles with DE = AD = 7 cm. Hence EC = DC − DE = 11 − 7 = 4 cm. (E does lie between D and C because 7 < 11.)"},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_006","question":"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = AC = 10$ cm and $BC = 12$ cm. $M$ is the midpoint of $BC$. Find the length of $AM$, in cm.","options":{},"correct_answer":"8","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["isosceles_triangle","pythagoras_theorem"],"common_errors":{"5":"Halving AB.","6":"Giving BM instead of AM.","15.62":"Using √(10² + 12²), i.e. treating the wrong pair as legs."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration: in an isosceles triangle the median to the base is also the altitude, so AM ⊥ BC. BM = 12/2 = 6 cm, and triangle ABM is right-angled at M: AM = √(10² − 6²) = √64 = 8 cm. Cross-check by area: Heron with s = 16 gives √(16×6×6×4) = √2304 = 48 cm², and ½ × 12 × AM = 48 gives AM = 8 ✓."},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_007","question":"In a circle of radius 13 cm, two parallel chords of lengths 24 cm and 10 cm are drawn on the same side of the centre. Find the distance between the two chords, in cm.","options":{"A":"7","B":"12","C":"17","D":"23"},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":135,"microskills":["circle_chord_properties","pythagoras_theorem"],"common_errors":{"B":"Giving the distance from the centre to the 10 cm chord only.","C":"Adding the two distances (5 + 12), which is the answer when the chords lie on opposite sides of the centre.","D":"Halving the chords and adding (12 + 5 + 6) or a similar mis-assembly."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration. Half-chords are 12 cm and 5 cm. Distance from the centre to the 24 cm chord: √(13² − 12²) = √25 = 5 cm. Distance from the centre to the 10 cm chord: √(13² − 5²) = √144 = 12 cm. Because both chords are on the same side of the centre, the distance between them is the difference: 12 − 5 = 7 cm. (On opposite sides it would be 12 + 5 = 17 cm — deciding which applies is the whole question.)"},{"id":"ISO_FIGURE_INTERPRETATION_008","question":"From an external point $P$, a tangent $PT$ is drawn to a circle with centre $O$ and radius 8 cm, touching the circle at $T$. Given that $OP = 17$ cm, find the length of $PT$, in cm.","options":{"A":"9","B":"15","C":"$\\\\sqrt{353}$","D":"25"},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"microskills":["circle_tangent_properties","pythagoras_theorem"],"common_errors":{"A":"Subtracting the radius from OP.","C":"Adding the squares instead of subtracting: √(289 + 64).","D":"Adding OP and the radius."},"target_procedural_skill":"FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","procedural_skills":["FIGURE_INTERPRETATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Read the configuration: a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, so ∠OTP = 90° and OTP is a right triangle with hypotenuse OP. PT = √(OP² − OT²) = √(289 − 64) = √225 = 15 cm. (8-15-17 is a Pythagorean triple ✓.)"},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_001","question":"What is the area of rectangle $ABCD$?<br>I. The perimeter of the rectangle is 40 cm.<br>II. The length of a diagonal of the rectangle is $\\\\sqrt{200}$ cm.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_rectangle","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"A":"Assuming a fixed perimeter pins down the area (it does not — a 15 × 5 and a 12 × 8 rectangle both have perimeter 40).","B":"Assuming a fixed diagonal pins down the area.","D":"Not spotting the identity (l + b)² = l² + b² + 2lb, which converts the pair into lb."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I gives l + b = 20; infinitely many rectangles satisfy this with different areas (15 × 5 = 75, 12 × 8 = 96), so I alone is insufficient. Statement II gives l² + b² = 200; again many rectangles qualify (10 × 10 and 14 × 2 both have l² + b² = 200), so II alone is insufficient. Together: (l + b)² = l² + b² + 2lb ⇒ 400 = 200 + 2lb ⇒ lb = 100, so the area is 100 cm² — a unique value. Answer: C. Note the sufficiency can be decided from the identity alone, without solving for l and b (they are in fact both 10)."},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_002","question":"Is the positive integer $n$ divisible by 12?<br>I. $n$ is divisible by 4.<br>II. $n$ is divisible by 6.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"C","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["divisibility","lcm","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"A":"Treating divisibility by 4 as enough (n = 8 is divisible by 4 but not by 12).","B":"Treating divisibility by 6 as enough (n = 6 is divisible by 6 but not by 12).","D":"Failing to see that a common multiple of 4 and 6 must be a multiple of their LCM, 12."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I alone: n = 4 gives 'no', n = 12 gives 'yes' — insufficient. Statement II alone: n = 6 gives 'no', n = 12 gives 'yes' — insufficient. Together: n is a common multiple of 4 and 6, hence a multiple of LCM(4, 6) = 12, so the answer is always 'yes'. A definite answer is obtained, so the statements together are sufficient. Answer: C. (A definite 'yes' counts as sufficiency; note the value of n itself is never determined, and does not need to be.)"},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_003","question":"What is the height of a right circular cone?<br>I. The volume of the cone is $100\\\\pi$ cm³.<br>II. The slant height of the cone is 13 cm and its base radius is 5 cm.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["cone_volume","pythagoras_theorem","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"A":"Assuming the volume alone fixes the height; it fixes only r²h = 300.","C":"Judging that II needs I as well, when II alone determines h = 12.","D":"Overlooking that slant height, radius and height form a right triangle."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I gives (1/3)πr²h = 100π, i.e. r²h = 300; the height is not determined (r = 10, h = 3 and r = 5, h = 12 both work), so I alone is insufficient. Statement II gives l = 13 and r = 5, and since l² = r² + h², h = √(169 − 25) = 12 cm — a unique value, so II alone is sufficient. Answer: B. (The statements are consistent: r = 5, h = 12 gives volume (1/3)π(25)(12) = 100π ✓.)"},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_004","question":"What is the two-digit positive integer $N$?<br>I. The sum of the digits of $N$ is 9.<br>II. $N$ is divisible by 9.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["divisibility_rules","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"A":"Assuming a digit sum of 9 identifies a unique two-digit number.","B":"Assuming divisibility by 9 identifies a unique two-digit number.","C":"Assuming the two statements narrow each other down, when they carry almost the same information."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I: the two-digit numbers with digit sum 9 are 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81, 90 — nine candidates, insufficient. Statement II: the two-digit multiples of 9 are those nine plus 99 — insufficient. Together: a two-digit number is divisible by 9 exactly when its digit sum is 9 or 18, so combining leaves the same nine candidates. Still not unique. Answer: D. The point is that the second statement is very nearly a restatement of the first and adds essentially nothing."},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_005","question":"What is the area of a rhombus?<br>I. Its diagonals have lengths 10 cm and 24 cm.<br>II. Its perimeter is 52 cm.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_rhombus","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"B":"Assuming the side length of a rhombus fixes its area; it does not, since the rhombus can be flattened.","C":"Judging that I needs II as well, when I alone gives ½ d₁d₂.","D":"Not recalling that a rhombus's area is half the product of its diagonals."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I alone: for a rhombus, area = ½ d₁d₂ = ½(10)(24) = 120 cm² — unique, so I is sufficient. Statement II alone: perimeter 52 gives side 13 cm, but a rhombus of side 13 can be anything from nearly flat (area → 0) to a square (area 169), so II is insufficient. Answer: A. (The statements are consistent: with diagonals 10 and 24, the half-diagonals 5 and 12 give side √(25 + 144) = 13 and perimeter 52 ✓.)"},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_006","question":"What is the remainder when the positive integer $n$ is divided by 5?<br>I. When $n$ is divided by 15, the remainder is 3.<br>II. $n$ is odd.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"A","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["remainders","divisibility","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"B":"Assuming parity constrains the remainder modulo 5.","C":"Judging that I needs II as well, when I alone forces the remainder to be 3.","D":"Not noticing that 5 divides 15, so a remainder mod 15 determines the remainder mod 5."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I: n = 15k + 3 for some integer k ≥ 0. Since 15k is a multiple of 5, n leaves remainder 3 on division by 5 — always, for every k. Sufficient. (Check: 3, 18, 33, 48 all leave remainder 3 mod 5.) Statement II: n odd allows n = 7 (remainder 2) and n = 9 (remainder 4) — insufficient. Answer: A. Sufficiency here follows from 5 | 15 alone; the value of n is never found."},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_007","question":"How many sides does a regular polygon have?<br>I. The sum of its exterior angles is $360^\\\\circ$.<br>II. Each of its interior angles measures $156^\\\\circ$.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"B","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["regular_polygon","interior_exterior_angles","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"A":"Treating statement I as informative, when the exterior angles of every convex polygon sum to 360°.","C":"Judging that II needs I as well, when II alone gives n = 15.","D":"Not recalling that interior and exterior angles at a vertex are supplementary."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I is true of every convex polygon regardless of the number of sides, so it carries no information — insufficient. Statement II: each exterior angle = 180° − 156° = 24°, and for a regular polygon n = 360/24 = 15 — unique, so II alone is sufficient. Answer: B. Recognising that a universally true statement is never sufficient is the skill being tested."},{"id":"ISO_SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION_008","question":"What is the area of triangle $ABC$?<br>I. $BC = 10$ cm.<br>II. The perimeter of the triangle is 36 cm.","options":{"A":"Statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement II alone is not.","B":"Statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, but statement I alone is not.","C":"Both statements together are sufficient, but neither statement alone is sufficient.","D":"Statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."},"correct_answer":"D","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"microskills":["area_of_triangle","herons_formula","data_sufficiency"],"common_errors":{"A":"Treating a known base as enough, with no height available.","B":"Treating the perimeter as enough to fix the area.","C":"Assuming base plus perimeter determines the triangle; it leaves AB + AC = 26 with the split free."},"target_procedural_skill":"SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","procedural_skills":["SUFFICIENCY_EVALUATION","FORMULA_RECALL"],"solution":"Statement I alone: base known, height unknown — insufficient. Statement II alone: many triangles share a perimeter of 36 with different areas — insufficient. Together: BC = 10 and AB + AC = 26, but the split is free. Taking AB = AC = 13 gives, by Heron with s = 18, area = √(18×8×5×5) = √3600 = 60 cm²; taking AB = 11, AC = 15 gives area = √(18×7×8×3) = √3024 ≈ 54.99 cm². Two different areas satisfy both statements, so together they are still insufficient. Answer: D. (Three sides would be needed — the perimeter alone is one equation short.)"}]`);export{e as default};
